\title{Reliable Intuition as a Non-Computable Resource:\\ Counting and Coding Barriers to a Ramanujan Oracle}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 11, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Many of Srinivasa Ramanujan's identities were announced without proof and only later verified, suggesting a model of mathematical \emph{intuition} as an \emph{oracle}: a device that, presented with an encoded number-theoretic statement, returns a verdict --- true or false --- reliably but without a proof. We formalize an oracle as a Boolean-valued function on encoded statements and establish two complementary obstructions to such an oracle being computable. The first is a \emph{cardinality obstruction}: by a diagonal argument the space of oracles admits no enumeration, whereas the computable oracles are countable, so a flawless oracle escapes any enumeration of programs. The second is a \emph{quantitative combinatorial obstruction}: on a block of $N$ statements, a single oracle predicts correctly to within $d$ errors exactly for those truth patterns lying in a Hamming ball of radius $d$, whose size is the binomial partial sum $\sum_{k \le d}\binom{N}{k}$; consequently any family of oracles whose size is small compared to $2^N / \sum_{k\le d}\binom{N}{k}$ is \emph{defeated} --- some truth pattern is mispredicted with more than $d$ errors by every oracle in the family. Setting $d = N - m$ recasts ``more than $d$ errors'' as ``accuracy below $m/N$,'' so a genuinely reliable oracle cannot be drawn from a small (for example, computably enumerable) pool. The bridge --- Hamming balls from coding theory controlling the reach of an oracle from computability theory --- is the cross-domain core. We close with conjectures relating reliable intuition to bi-immunity and to the Turing-jump hierarchy.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

In 1913, Ramanujan sent G.~H.~Hardy a series of letters containing dozens of identities in number theory and analysis, most stated without proof. A striking proportion turned out to be correct, their proofs supplied only later and often by others. This historical pattern motivates a question that can be made mathematically precise: if we model ``intuition'' as a reliable but proof-free verdict map on statements, can that map be an algorithm?

We answer in the negative, twice over. The two arguments are independent and illuminate different facets of the phenomenon.

\begin{itemize}
\item A \emph{soft, cardinality-based} argument (Sections~\ref{sec:cantor}--\ref{sec:escape}) shows that oracles are too numerous to enumerate, whereas programs are countable; hence a perfect oracle need not be computable, and indeed a diagonal construction exhibits, for any enumeration, a truth assignment whose perfect oracle escapes it.
\item A \emph{hard, quantitative} argument (Section~\ref{sec:barrier}) shows that even bounded-error reliability on a \emph{finite} block forces an oracle into a small Hamming ball, so that any small family of oracles fails to cover all truth patterns. This turns non-computability into a concrete counting inequality with no appeal to infinity.
\end{itemize}

Throughout, statements are encoded as natural numbers, and an oracle (or truth assignment) is a function assigning a Boolean verdict to each encoded statement.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Statement encoding]
Every mathematical statement is a finite string over a finite alphabet, and finite strings are placed in computable bijection with the natural numbers $\N = \{0,1,2,\dots\}$. We identify the set of statements with $\N$ and write statement $n$ for the statement encoded by $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Oracle]
An \emph{oracle} is a function $R : \N \to \{\text{false}, \text{true}\}$, equivalently a map $\N \to \{0,1\}$. The same type of object serves as a \emph{truth assignment} $T$, interpreted as the ground truth: $T(n)$ is the actual truth value of statement $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Accuracy and error]
On a finite block of statements indexed by $\{0,\dots,N-1\}$, both an oracle $r$ and the truth $t$ restrict to strings in $\{0,1\}^N$. The \emph{Hamming distance} $\dist(r,t)$ is the number of positions where they disagree. The oracle makes $d$ errors if $\dist(r,t) = d$; its \emph{accuracy} is $(N - \dist(r,t))/N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Computable oracle]
An oracle $R$ is \emph{computable} if some algorithm, on input $n$, halts and outputs $R(n)$. Fixing a standard enumeration of programs induces an enumeration $\text{enum} : \N \to (\N \to \{0,1\})$ whose range contains the computable oracles; in particular they are countable. We model ``the computable oracles,'' or any listable pool of methods, abstractly as the range of a fixed enumeration $\text{enum}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hamming ball]
For $N,d \in \N$ and a center $r \in \{0,1\}^N$, the \emph{Hamming ball} of radius $d$ is $B_d(r) = \{\, t \in \{0,1\}^N : \dist(r,t) \le d \,\}$.
\end{definition}

\section{The space of oracles is not enumerable}\label{sec:cantor}

\begin{theorem}[No enumeration of oracles]
There is no surjection $f : \N \to (\N \to \{0,1\})$. No single list $R_0, R_1, R_2, \dots$ contains every oracle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $f$ were such a surjection; write $R_n = f(n)$. Define $g(n) = \operatorname{not}(R_n(n))$. By surjectivity there is $m$ with $f(m) = g$, hence $g(m) = R_m(m)$. But $g(m) = \operatorname{not}(R_m(m))$, so $R_m(m) = \operatorname{not}(R_m(m))$, impossible for a Boolean value.
\end{proof}

The space of oracles is uncountable: intuition ranges over uncountably many possible verdict-functions.

\section{A perfect oracle escapes any enumeration of programs}\label{sec:escape}

Fix any enumeration $\text{enum} : \N \to (\N \to \{0,1\})$.

\begin{definition}[Diagonal oracle]
The \emph{diagonal oracle} of $\text{enum}$ is $D(n) = \operatorname{not}(\text{enum}(n)(n))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Diagonal disagreement]
For every $n$, $D \ne \text{enum}(n)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $D = \text{enum}(n)$, evaluating at $n$ gives $D(n) = \text{enum}(n)(n)$; but $D(n) = \operatorname{not}(\text{enum}(n)(n))$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Diagonal not in range]
$D \notin \operatorname{range}(\text{enum})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $D = \text{enum}(n)$ for some $n$, the previous lemma is violated at that $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A perfect oracle escapes]
For every enumeration $\text{enum}$ there exists a truth assignment $T$ (namely $T = D$) whose flawless oracle is not in $\operatorname{range}(\text{enum})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Non-computability by counting]
Modeling the computable oracles as $\operatorname{range}(\text{enum})$, there is a truth assignment whose perfect oracle is not computable. A flawless intuition cannot be captured by any enumeration of algorithms.
\end{corollary}

This argument is decisive but qualitative: it requires perfection and reasons about the infinite domain $\N$. The next section removes both limitations.

\section{The quantitative accuracy barrier}\label{sec:barrier}

We fix a finite block of $N$ statements, identifying oracles and truths with elements of $\{0,1\}^N$.

\subsection{The size of a Hamming ball}

\begin{lemma}[Subset counting]
The number of subsets of an $N$-element set with at most $d$ elements is $\sum_{k=0}^{d}\binom{N}{k}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Partition the subsets of size $\le d$ by cardinality $k \in \{0,\dots,d\}$. Those of size exactly $k$ number $\binom{N}{k}$, and the classes are disjoint; summing gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ball-size formula]
For any center $r \in \{0,1\}^N$ and any $d$,
\[
|B_d(r)| = \sum_{k=0}^{d}\binom{N}{k},
\]
independent of the center $r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Map each $t \in B_d(r)$ to its disagreement set $S(t) = \{ i : r_i \ne t_i \}$, of size $\dist(r,t) \le d$. This is a bijection onto the subsets of size $\le d$: it is injective since $t$ is recovered from $r$ by flipping the coordinates in $S(t)$, and surjective since flipping $r$ on any subset $S$ of size $\le d$ yields a point of $B_d(r)$ with disagreement set $S$. Apply the subset-counting lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cube size]
$|\{0,1\}^N| = 2^N$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Proper balls are strictly smaller]\label{lem:proper}
If $d < N$ then $|B_d(r)| < 2^N$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By $\sum_{k=0}^N \binom{N}{k} = 2^N$, splitting at $d$ gives $2^N = \sum_{k\le d}\binom{N}{k} + \sum_{k=d+1}^N \binom{N}{k}$; the second sum contains $\binom{N}{d+1} > 0$ since $d < N$, so the first is strictly less than $2^N$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Small families are defeated}

\begin{theorem}[Accuracy barrier]\label{thm:barrier}
Fix $N$ and an error budget $d < N$. Let $F \subseteq \{0,1\}^N$ be a finite family with
\[
|F| \cdot \sum_{k=0}^{d}\binom{N}{k} < 2^N.
\]
Then there exists $t \in \{0,1\}^N$ with $\dist(r,t) > d$ for every $r \in F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\text{covered} = \bigcup_{r \in F} B_d(r)$. By the union bound and the ball-size formula,
\[
|\text{covered}| \le \sum_{r \in F} |B_d(r)| = |F| \cdot \sum_{k=0}^d \binom{N}{k} < 2^N = |\{0,1\}^N|.
\]
Hence some $t \in \{0,1\}^N$ is uncovered, i.e. $t \notin B_d(r)$ for all $r \in F$, meaning $\dist(r,t) > d$ for every $r \in F$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Reliability requires a large pool]
With accuracy $m/N$ and $m = N - d$: if $|F| < 2^N / \sum_{k=0}^{N-m}\binom{N}{k}$, then some truth pattern is predicted with accuracy strictly below $m/N$ by every oracle in $F$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[The $95\%$ barrier]
Take $m = \lceil 0.95 N \rceil$, so $d \approx 0.05 N$. Since $\sum_{k \le 0.05N}\binom{N}{k} = 2^{H(0.05)N + o(N)}$ with $H(0.05) \approx 0.286$, the covering threshold is $\approx 2^{0.714 N}$, exponential in $N$. Any family of sub-exponential size --- in particular any pool from a fixed finite prefix of a program enumeration --- is eventually defeated.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Why one-half is the threshold}

The binary entropy $H(p) = -p\log_2 p - (1-p)\log_2(1-p)$ governs the growth $\sum_{k\le pN}\binom{N}{k} = 2^{H(p)N + o(N)}$ for $p \le 1/2$. As $H(1/2) = 1$, a ball of radius $N/2$ fills essentially the whole cube and accuracy $1/2$ is free. For $\alpha > 1/2$ the radius $(1-\alpha)N < N/2$ gives $H(1-\alpha) < 1$ and covering threshold $2^{(1 - H(1-\alpha))N}$, exponentially large. The transition occurs exactly at $\alpha = 1/2$.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Ball-size and threshold.} Given $N,d$, compute $\sum_{k\le d}\binom{N}{k}$ by an $O(d)$ recurrence on binomial coefficients and compare $|F|\cdot\sum_{k\le d}\binom{N}{k}$ against $2^N$ to certify defeat.

\smallskip
\textbf{Diagonal oracle.} With access to $\text{enum}$, compute $D(n) = \operatorname{not}(\text{enum}(n)(n))$ pointwise, querying one listed oracle per input.

\smallskip
\textbf{Uncovered-pattern search.} Given explicit $F$ below threshold, enumerate the $2^N$ patterns and return the first $t$ with $\dist(r,t) > d$ for all $r \in F$; Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier} guarantees success.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

Ramanujan's practice --- reliable verdicts without derivations --- is exactly an accurate oracle, and our results say such reliability is a non-algorithmic resource. Any finite ensemble of heuristics or learned models is a family $F$, so the barrier caps the worst-case accuracy it can guarantee. In coding-theoretic terms an oracle is a codeword, predicting within $d$ errors is decoding within radius $d$, and a family covering all patterns is a covering code; the accuracy barrier is the sphere-covering bound.

\section{Discussion}

The cardinality obstruction is clean and absolute but requires perfection and infinity. The counting obstruction is finite, quantitative, and robust to the notion of ``small pool,'' at the cost of speaking about worst-case truth patterns. Together they argue that reliable, proof-free mathematical intuition, in the strong worst-case sense, cannot be an algorithm drawn from any list we could write.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{A sharp accuracy--enumeration tradeoff.} For every $\alpha \in (1/2,1)$ there should be a truth assignment such that no uniformly enumerated family of oracles of subexponential growth attains asymptotic accuracy $\alpha$ on length-$N$ prefixes as $N \to \infty$, failing exactly at $\alpha = 1/2$. The count $\sum_{k\le(1-\alpha)N}\binom{N}{k}$ collapses super-polynomially once $\alpha > 1/2$; the finite covering threshold proved here is the quantity to promote to an asymptotic density statement.

\smallskip
\textbf{Bi-immune truth assignments.} There should exist truth assignments whose prefixes cannot be predicted by any single computable oracle with accuracy bounded away from $1/2$ on a positive-density set of prefixes. Combining the per-block ball bound with diagonalization should yield approximation-resistant functions.

\smallskip
\textbf{Intuition and the jump hierarchy.} Grading truth assignments by the least oracle-degree predicting their prefixes with accuracy $\alpha \to 1$ should induce a hierarchy interleaving with the Turing-jump hierarchy, so the ``intuitive leap'' is a graded resource with higher reliability costing strictly more oracle power.

\section{Conclusion}

Reliable Ramanujan-style intuition cannot be computable: the oracles are uncountable while programs are countable (a diagonal argument), and reliability confines an oracle to a Hamming ball of size $\sum_{k\le d}\binom{N}{k}$ so small pools are defeated (a covering argument). The bridge between coding theory and computability locates the intuitive leap just beyond the countable world of algorithms, on the far side of the one-half accuracy cliff.

